"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][1]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M1-L1 – Charge, Current, Voltage and Power<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Introductory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Basic fractions, powers of ten and\nunit conversion. The slope of a straight line is covered in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">School\nMathematics M1-L8<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish charge, current,\nvoltage, power and energy, convert their units and determine the\ndirection of energy transfer at a two-terminal element.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two Measurements, One Question<\/h2>\n<p>A simulated device shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat its terminals. Is the device absorbing energy? The numbers alone\ncannot answer the question until we know <strong>which terminal is at\nthe higher electric potential<\/strong> and <strong>in which direction\ncurrent is counted<\/strong>. The same measured voltage can occur at a\nsource delivering energy or at a load absorbing energy.<\/p>\n<p>This first lesson introduces the quantities and their signs using a\n<strong>two-terminal element<\/strong>, a component or system with\nexactly two terminals under consideration. In the next lesson, the\nresistor provides a specific constitutive law for such an element.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Charge and Current<\/h2>\n<p>Electric charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in coulombs\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThe elementary charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the positive magnitude of the electron’s charge. An electron has\ncharge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts exact numerical value is defined in the International System of\nUnits:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>176<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>634<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=1.602\\,176\\,634\\cdot 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Imagine a cross-section in the conductor. First choose a positive\ndirection. The quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{tr}}(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the <strong>net charge transferred in this direction<\/strong>\nsince a reference instant. The average current over an interval and the\ninstantaneous current are then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>i<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{i}=\\frac{\\Delta q_{\\mathrm{tr}}}{\\Delta t},\n\\qquad\ni(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm d q_{\\mathrm{tr}}}{\\mathrm dt}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>One ampere equals one coulomb per second. If a net charge of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.40\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npasses in the positive reference direction in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe average current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative result represents charge transport opposite to the defined\nconventional current direction. In a metallic conductor, the negatively\ncharged electrons move on average opposite to conventional current.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"current-and-stored-charge\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Current and Stored Charge<\/h3>\n<p>Current describes transport across a boundary. Charge within a volume\nis a different quantity. For example, if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nenters a region and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleaves, its enclosed net charge does not change as a result. This is the\nidea behind Kirchhoff’s current law in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Voltage Is a Difference<\/h2>\n<p>The voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured from terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the electrostatic or lumped network model used here, the potentials\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobey<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}=v_a-v_b.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>One volt equals one joule per coulomb. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}=6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe potential at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhigher than at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf positive charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis moved from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits potential energy changes by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=q\\,u_{ab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMoving the same charge in the opposite direction reverses the sign. A\nreference node can be assigned\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout changing any voltage difference.<\/p>\n<p>In a circuit diagram,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>−<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicate the <strong>chosen reference polarity<\/strong>. A calculation\nmay yield a negative value. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_a=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_b=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}=-3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ba}=+3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power and Energy<\/h2>\n<p>Power describes energy transfer per unit time. Under the\n<strong>passive sign convention<\/strong>, the positive current arrow\npoints into the terminal marked\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the chosen voltage. The absorbed power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the net absorbed energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover an interval are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=u(t)\\,i(t),\n\\qquad\nE_{[t_1,t_2]}=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}p(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Positive power means absorption; negative power means delivery. The\nrule also applies when the substituted\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis negative. The units agree, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V}\\,\\mathrm{A}=(\\mathrm{J}/\\mathrm C)(\\mathrm C/\\mathrm s)=\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor constant current and voltage,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=p\\,\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne watt-second is one joule; one watt-hour is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>600<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,600<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\njoules.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_001_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two two-terminal elements with the same voltage and current magnitude. On the left, current enters the positive terminal and power is absorbed. On the right, current leaves the positive terminal and power is delivered.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At 6 V and 0.20 A, current entering the positive\nterminal means +1.20 W absorbed on the left; current leaving gives −1.20\nW on the right.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In the right-hand element, the current arrow shows the actual\ndirection. Relative to the passive convention, its current is negative.\nAlternatively, one can choose a reference arrow pointing out of the\npositive terminal. In that convention the formula for absorbed power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=-ui<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth representations give the same energy balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Energy Transfer in a Model Device<\/h2>\n<p>A constant voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexists between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe reference voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nenters terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model device therefore absorbs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn one minute,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>72<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1.20\\,\\mathrm W\\cdot 60\\,\\mathrm s=72\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>is transferred to the two-terminal element. At the same current\nmagnitude,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof net charge leaves one terminal and enters the other during this time.\nThe device therefore does not necessarily store\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof charge. The fact that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nestablishes only net energy absorption. What happens to the energy\ninternally depends on the component model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A net charge of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm{mC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis transferred in the positive current direction over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">250\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the average current.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{ab}=+9\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncurrent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows out of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine absorbed power under the passive convention and the net energy\ntransferred after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A load absorbs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring the first second and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring the second. How much energy does it absorb in total, and what is\nits average power?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>i<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.015<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.060<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline i=0.015\\,\\mathrm C/0.250\\,\\mathrm s=0.060\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The current is negative relative to the direction into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=9\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot(-0.30\\,\\mathrm A)=-2.7\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=-54\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two-terminal element delivers a net\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">54\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=3\\,\\mathrm J+1\\,\\mathrm J=4\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAverage power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm J/2\\,\\mathrm s=2\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Next Step<\/h2>\n<p>The model treats terminals and connections as lumped elements. At\nthis stage it describes neither spatial field distributions nor\npropagation times along a line. In the following examples, clearly\ndefined terminal quantities, reference directions and the balance across\nthe model boundary are therefore essential.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2 –\nResistance, Ohm’s Law and Simple Networks<\/a> relates voltage and\ncurrent through a constitutive law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.bipm.org/en/publications/si-brochure\">BIPM – SI\nBrochure, 9th edition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · chapter 2, definition of the ampere and\nderived SI units. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 9.1 – Electrical Current<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Shared\nsource context: definition of current and relationship to power. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-5-electrical-energy-and-power\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 9.5 – Electrical Energy and Power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nShared source context: definition of current and relationship to power.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 – Circuits and Electronics, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\ncourse on lumped modelling and elementary circuit analysis, lectures 1–2\nin the lecture notes. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M1-L2 – Resistance, Ohm's Law and Simple Networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Introductory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a>,\nparticularly voltage, direction of power transfer and units.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate currents, voltages and\npowers from the linear resistor model, recognise series and parallel\nconnections, and explain the behaviour of a loaded voltage divider.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Same Source, Different Output Voltage<\/h2>\n<p>Two resistors are connected to an ideal voltage source. At their\ncommon node, a defined fraction of the source voltage can be obtained.\nIf you connect another device to this node, the output voltage may\nchange. This change is due to <strong>loading<\/strong>. To calculate it,\nyou only need a constitutive law and information about which resistors\nare connected via the same nodes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ohmic Resistance and the Material Model<\/h2>\n<p>For a linear, time-invariant ohmic resistor under the passive sign\nconvention,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=R\\,i,\n\\qquad\n[R]=\\Omega=\\frac{\\mathrm V}{\\mathrm A}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the slope of a plot of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf you instead plot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe slope is the conductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=1/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin siemens\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nBoth describe the same characteristic. The power absorbed by the\nresistor is non-negative for either current direction:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=ui=R i^2=\\frac{u^2}{R}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a straight, homogeneous conductor of uniform cross-sectional area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\rho\\ell/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a simple material model. Resistivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTemperature, contacts and non-uniform geometry may alter this model. A\ndiode or a strongly heated wire cannot adequately be described by a\nsingle constant slope throughout its characteristic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Series and Parallel Connections<\/h2>\n<p>The same current passes through all elements in series if the common\nnode has no other connection. The individual voltages add with\nconsistent reference directions. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k=R_k i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{series}}=\\sum_k R_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Elements are connected in parallel if their terminals are connected\nto the same pair of nodes. They have the same voltage, and the branch\ncurrents add. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_k=u/R_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∥<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1}{R_{\\parallel}}=\\sum_k\\frac{1}{R_k},\n\\qquad\nR_1\\parallel R_2=\\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For two finite positive resistances,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\parallel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis smaller than either individual resistance. This property is a quick\ncheck for calculation and classification errors. The geometrical\npositions of the symbols in a circuit diagram do not determine the\nconnection type. The electrical nodes do.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Loaded Voltage Divider<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal source provides\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\mathrm{in}=12\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe upper resistor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the lower one is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2=4\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe measure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\mathrm{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the intermediate node relative to the reference node.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_002_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Voltage divider with a twelve-volt input, two kilohms at the top and four kilohms at the bottom. A switchable four-kilohm load resistor is connected in parallel with the lower resistor.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The 12 V divider with 2 kΩ above and 4 kΩ below\nprovides 8 V unloaded; a parallel 4 kΩ load reduces its output to 6\nV.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><strong>Unloaded.<\/strong> The series current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm V/(6\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)=2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage across the lower resistor is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn general,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\mathrm{out}=u_\\mathrm{in}R_2/(R_1+R_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas long as the output has no additional supplying or load branch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Loaded with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L=4\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in parallel with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir equivalent resistance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe source now sees a series combination of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega+2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSource current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the output falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Voltage magnitude<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Current magnitude<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Absorbed power<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Upper resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The ideal source delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot3\\,\\mathrm{mA}=36\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether the three resistors absorb the same power. In the limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe unloaded output is recovered. As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\mathrm{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches zero. In this idealised example, input current remains\nlimited by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two resistors,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nare connected in parallel across\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate equivalent resistance, branch currents and total\ndissipation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An unloaded divider with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=R_2=10\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis connected to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA measuring instrument is modelled by an input resistance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat voltage does the model indicate, and how far does it deviate from\nthe intended unloaded value?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two resistors share a node to which a current source is also\nconnected. Is the common node sufficient to establish a series\nconnection?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\parallel=200\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe branch currents are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a total current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.18\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The lower equivalent resistance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.091<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(100/11)\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega\\approx9.091\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>21<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.762<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100/21\\,\\mathrm V\\approx4.762\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe deviation is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.238\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4.76<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4.76\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. The third branch permits different currents in the two\nresistors. The shared node alone does not guarantee equal currents.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Next Step<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation uses ideal wires, an ideal voltage source and linear,\ntime-invariant resistors. Real measurements may differ from the model\ndue to temperature, tolerances, source impedance and additional\nelectrical effects. Even in the ideal model, the loaded divider shows\nwhy the input of the next device is part of the circuit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 –\nKirchhoff’s Laws and Systematic Network Analysis<\/a> examines networks\nthat cannot be solved simply by combining series and parallel\nconnections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 9.3 – Resistivity and Resistance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nShared source context: resistor model and network reduction. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 9.4 – Ohm’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 9.4 –\nShared source context: resistor model and network reduction. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-2-resistors-in-series-and-parallel\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.2 – Resistors in Series and\nParallel<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.2 – Shared source context: resistor model and\nnetwork reduction. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 – Circuits and Electronics, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nlecture 2 in the lecture notes. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M1-L3 – Kirchhoff's Laws and Systematic Network Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Charge,\nCurrent, Voltage and Power<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Resistance,\nOhm’s Law and Simple Networks<\/a>. The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">lesson\non systems of linear equations<\/a> helps with solving equations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define current and voltage\nsigns, analyse a network with two unknown node potentials, and verify\nthe result using mesh equations and the power balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a Circuit to an Equation<\/h2>\n<p>Two resistors can only be combined directly in series if the same\ncurrent flows through both. As soon as another branch leaves their\njunction, this condition must first be checked. Kirchhoff’s laws provide\na procedure that also works for multiple branches. The connections are\ndescribed using node and loop equations, supplemented by the\nconstitutive relations of the components.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>node<\/strong> includes all points directly connected by\nideal wires. A <strong>branch<\/strong> connects two nodes through a\ncomponent or an unbranched chain of components. A <strong>closed\nloop<\/strong> follows branches back to its starting point. In a planar\ncircuit drawing, closed loops that enclose no other loop are called\nmeshes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Current Law and Voltage Law<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Kirchhoff’s current law (KCL).<\/strong> At an ideal node with\nno charge storage of its own, the algebraic sum of currents is zero. If\ncurrents leaving the node are taken as positive, currents entering it\nhave a negative sign. At the next node, the same branch current may\nappear with the opposite sign.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_k I_k = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Kirchhoff’s voltage law (KVL).<\/strong> As you traverse a\nloop, add the potential changes. A step from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>−<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross a component contributes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe reverse step contributes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince the starting and ending points coincide,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_k \\Delta V_k = 0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A reference node is assigned\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{ab}=V_a-V_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand assumed current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{ab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefer to the same ordered pair of nodes. For a resistor from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{ab}=(V_a-V_b)/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative result indicates that actual conventional current flows\nopposite to the selected arrow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nodal Analysis of a Two-Node Network<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal source maintains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_S=14\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_3=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_4=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth unknown potentials are specified relative to the same reference\nnode.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_003_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Network with a 14 V source, two unknown node potentials and four resistors. Labelled arrows show the four calculated branch currents.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the branched 14 V network, 6 mA reach node A and\nsplit into 4 mA downwards and 2 mA onwards to node B.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For each unknown node, write down the sum of the outgoing\ncurrents.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Replace resistor currents with potential differences divided by\nresistance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solve the system of equations and calculate branch currents from the\npotentials.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>A<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>B<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nA:\\quad\n\\frac{V_A-14\\,\\mathrm{V}}{1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}+\n\\frac{V_A}{2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}+\n\\frac{V_A-V_B}{2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}&amp;=0,\\\\\nB:\\quad\n\\frac{V_B-V_A}{2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}+\n\\frac{V_B}{2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}&amp;=0.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Multiplying by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4V_A-V_B=28\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2V_B=V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A=8\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_B=4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch and positive reference direction<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Current<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Absorbed power<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\to 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Loop check.<\/strong> Along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\to S\\to A\\to 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe potential changes are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+14-6-8)\\,\\mathrm V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\to A\\to B\\to 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthey are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+8-4-4)\\,\\mathrm V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Power check.<\/strong> For each resistor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=I^2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe source delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>84<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot6\\,\\mathrm{mA}=84\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts current leaves the positive terminal. In the sign convention for\nabsorbed power, it therefore contributes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>84<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-84\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIncluding the resistors gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>84<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-84+36+32+8+8=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin milliwatts.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"second-method-mesh-currents\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Second Method: Mesh Currents<\/h3>\n<p>Choose two clockwise mesh currents\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn the left,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npasses through the source and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non the right,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npasses through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the shared branch\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe current from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the reference is the difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1-j_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nKirchhoff’s voltage law gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n(3\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)j_1-(2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)j_2&amp;=14\\,\\mathrm V,\\\\\n-(2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)j_1+(6\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)j_2&amp;=0.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2=j_1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_1=6\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j_2=2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAgain,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor this network, both approaches yield two equations. Which method is\nshorter depends on how many node or mesh variables are needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ideal Voltage Source Between Two Unknown Nodes<\/h2>\n<p>The current through an ideal voltage source cannot be inferred from\nits known voltage. If the source lies between two unknown nodes, the two\ncan be treated together as a <strong>supernode<\/strong>. The current\nbalance considers only the branches crossing its boundary. The unknown\nsource current flows within the boundary and cancels from the combined\nbalance. The second equation is the specified voltage difference between\nthe two nodes. The source current can then be calculated using KCL at\neither individual node.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Remove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the two-node network. How do\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{AB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal current source injects\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNodes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare connected to the reference node via\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. An ideal voltage source imposes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A-V_B=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the current through the voltage source in direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Only one branch remains connected to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nKCL requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{AB}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_B=V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nform the remaining voltage divider. Both nodes are therefore at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot 2/3\\approx9.33\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the supernode,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A/(2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})+V_B/(4\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})=4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2V_A+V_B=16\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether with the voltage constraint, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_A=6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_B=4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOf the injected\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows through the resistor from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the reference. The remaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows through the voltage source from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope of Validity and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The model treats wires as ideal connections and concentrates voltage\ndrops and storage effects in components. Where node capacitance is\nsignificant, its storage current can be modelled as a separate branch.\nWhen time-dependent magnetic flux passes through a wire loop, induction\nmust be accounted for, for example as an induced voltage in an\nappropriate branch model. In spatially extended networks and with fast\nsignals, propagation delays may also be important.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Equivalent\nCircuits and Maximum Power Transfer<\/a> uses the same equations to\ndescribe a network at two terminals using a simpler model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/resources/lecture-2/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 Circuits and Electronics, lecture 2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsystematic circuit analysis; checked for the German original on 19\nNovember 2026. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.3 – current law,\nvoltage law and power balance; checked for the German original on 19\nNovember 2026. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M1-L4 – Equivalent Circuits and Maximum Power Transfer<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kirchhoff’s\nLaws and Systematic Network Analysis<\/a>, voltage dividers and\nelectrical power.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe a linear two-terminal\nnetwork as a Thévenin or Norton source, determine its internal\nresistance even in the presence of dependent sources, and calculate the\nload resistance for maximum load power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What Can We Observe at the Terminals of a Network?<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor may behave differently at its two output terminals as soon\nas a downstream device draws current. The open-circuit voltage describes\nonly the case in which the output is open. The loaded voltage also\ndepends on how much it falls as the load current increases.<\/p>\n<p>For the following DC models, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=V_a-V_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleaves the network at terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand flows through the load from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a linear two-terminal network with finite internal resistance, the\ncharacteristic<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=U_0-R_\\mathrm{i} I_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>describes the terminal quantities.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the open-circuit voltage and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the slope of the characteristic under this sign convention. The\n<strong>Thévenin equivalent circuit<\/strong> consists of an ideal\nvoltage source\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin series with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}\\ne 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe corresponding <strong>Norton equivalent circuit<\/strong> has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin parallel with an ideal current source\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm{N}=U_0/R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts reference direction within the equivalent network is from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe external short-circuit current in the chosen load direction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L=I_\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Source Contributions, Internal Resistance and Test\nSources<\/h2>\n<p>With <strong>superposition<\/strong>, we calculate the contribution of\neach independent source and add the resulting voltages or currents with\ntheir signs. In each subcircuit, the source under consideration and all\ndependent sources remain active. All other independent sources are set\nto zero. An ideal voltage source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to a short circuit, and an ideal current source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto an open circuit. Components and internal resistances of the original\ncircuit remain in place.<\/p>\n<p>Power is calculated only after combining the currents and voltages. A\nresistor alone shows why individual power contributions cannot generally\nbe added. The expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(I_1+I_2)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains the additional cross term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2RI_1I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>To find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nremove the load and set all independent sources to zero. If the\nremaining network consists of resistors, it can often be evaluated using\nseries and parallel combinations. Dependent sources remain active in\nthis step as well. We can then apply a test voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{T}=V_a-V_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand calculate the current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninjected into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby the test source. With this reference current directed <strong>into\nthe network<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}=U_\\mathrm{T}/I_\\mathrm{T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the quotient is defined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Voltage Divider Supplies a Load<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeeds output terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=3\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nResistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2=6\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconnects\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto reference terminal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load is also connected between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Open circuit.<\/strong> With the load removed, the voltage\ndivider gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0=15\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot 6/(3+6)=10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Internal resistance.<\/strong> After setting the ideal\nvoltage source to zero,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare in parallel as seen from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Norton current.<\/strong> From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0/R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs a check, the original model at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsupplies the same current through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile no current flows through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Loading.<\/strong> With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L=\\frac{10\\,\\mathrm V}{(2+2)\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}=2.5\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_L=12.5\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the original network, the parallel combination of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreduces the effective resistance to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage-divider calculation likewise gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Maximum Load Power and Efficiency<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0\\ne 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_L=I_L^2R_L\n=\\frac{U_0^2R_L}{(R_\\mathrm{i}+R_L)^2}\n=\\frac{U_0^2}{4R_\\mathrm{i}+(R_\\mathrm{i}-R_L)^2/R_L}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The denominator is smallest exactly when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L=R_\\mathrm{i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn our example, the maximum load power is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{(10\\,\\mathrm V)^2}{4\\cdot2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}}=12.5\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>In the Thévenin equivalent model, both resistors dissipate the same\npower when matched. The load receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the power supplied by the ideal equivalent voltage source. To\ndetermine the efficiency of the original network, its internal losses\nmust be calculated. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe original\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsource supplies a current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac{10}{3}\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor a total of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this network,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12.5\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreaches the load, corresponding to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_113_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Load power versus load resistance, with a maximum of 12.5 milliwatts at 2 kilohms.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Load power in the Thévenin equivalent reaches a\nmaximum of 12.5 mW at a 2 kΩ load, which does not maximise overall\nefficiency.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The curve represents the load-power formula for the worked example.\nLoad power decreases for very small and very large load resistances; the\nmaximum occurs when the resistances are matched.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add an independent ideal current source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the voltage divider. Keep the load disconnected. Calculate the new\nopen-circuit voltage using superposition and state the internal\nresistance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresistor, an independent current source of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a dependent current source are connected in parallel between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe latter carries current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=0.25\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine the internal resistance using a test voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The voltage source contributes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the current-source contribution, the zero-valued voltage source is\nreplaced by a short circuit. In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∥<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1\\parallel R_2=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe injected current produces an additional\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen both independent sources are set to zero,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}=2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With the independent current source open-circuited, the test\nproduces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough the resistor and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough the still-active dependent source. The total current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{i}=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn open-circuit operation, the independent source injects exactly these\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0=4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Linear equivalent models provide the terminal quantities for\npermissible loads. Assessing stresses on internal components requires\nthe original network. For sources with current limiting or other\nnonlinear components, a linear approximation is valid only within a\njustified operating range. The short-circuit values here are results of\nidealised circuit calculations. The maximum-power condition in this\nlesson assumes a positive real internal resistance and a purely\nresistive load.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Capacitors,\nInductors and Transient Responses<\/a> introduces components that\ntemporarily store energy. There, the equivalent resistance seen from the\nstorage elements helps determine the time constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/resources/lecture-3/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 Circuits and Electronics, Lecture 3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsuperposition and two-terminal equivalent circuits · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/sites.coecis.cornell.edu/dist/4/81/files/2019/06/ECE2100-L8-notes.pdf\">Cornell\nUniversity – ECE/ENGRD 2100, Lecture 8 (PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Slides 9–10, Maximum\nPower Transfer: matched load and 50% efficiency at maximum power. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-5-electrical-energy-and-power\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 9.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · electrical energy and power for\nresistive loads · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M1-L5 – Capacitors, Inductors and Transient Responses<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kirchhoff’s\nLaws<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">equivalent\ncircuits<\/a> and differentiation of exponential functions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the initial value,\nfinal value and time constant of a first-order circuit and use them to\ncalculate a step response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Circuit with Memory<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor signal jumps from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter a resistor and capacitor, the same change appears with a delay.\nHow long does it take for the output to approach the new value? To\nanswer this, we need an <strong>initial condition<\/strong>. It describes\nthe energy already stored in the circuit at the instant of\nswitching.<\/p>\n<p>We use the passive sign convention for each component. Positive\ncurrent enters at the terminal where the voltage is measured as\npositive. For an ideal capacitor with constant capacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an ideal inductor with constant inductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C(t)=C\\frac{\\mathrm d u_C}{\\mathrm d t},\\qquad\nu_L(t)=L\\frac{\\mathrm d i_L}{\\mathrm d t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The stored energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_C=Cu_C^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><msubsup><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_L=Li_L^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. With finite capacitor current,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains continuous during switching. With finite inductor voltage,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains continuous. The other quantity of each component can jump. In\nthe ideal model, a jump in the storage variable would require a current\nor voltage impulse, respectively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Step Response of an RC Circuit<\/h2>\n<p>A source of constant voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndrives a resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin series with a capacitor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstarting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWrite the loop equation and replace the current with the capacitor\nequation. This gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s=Ri_C+u_C,\\qquad\nRC\\frac{\\mathrm d u_C}{\\mathrm d t}+u_C=U_s.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The steady-state final value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_\\infty=U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause no current flows when the capacitor voltage is constant. For the\ndeviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=u_C-U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dx/\\mathrm dt=-x/(RC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0^+)=u_C(0^-)-U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C(t)=U_s+\\bigl(u_C(0^-)-U_s\\bigr)\\mathrm e^{-t/\\tau},\n\\qquad \\tau=RC,\\quad t\\ge0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>After one time constant, the fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the initial deviation remains. A charging curve has therefore\ncompleted about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>63.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">63.2\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>of the change<\/strong>. After five time constants, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.67\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example and Numerical Experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=100\\,\\mu\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C(0^-)=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe time constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5-2)\\,\\mathrm V/(1\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)=3\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.896<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C=5\\,\\mathrm V-3\\,\\mathrm V/\\mathrm e\\approx3.896\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curve approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom below.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_004_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"RC charging curve from 2 V towards 5 V. After 100 ms the step response reaches approximately 3.90 V.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: After the voltage step, capacitor voltage rises\nasymptotically from 2 V to 5 V; at 100 ms it is about 3.90\nV.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A numerical approximation uses the slope at the beginning of each\ntime step. The following Python program compares this <strong>explicit\nEuler method<\/strong> with the closed-form solution. Both time-step\nsizes produce a stable, monotonic response here. The smaller step gives\na more accurate curve at the indicated times.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> exp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>tau <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u_start <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u_final <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">5.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> dt <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.01<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.001<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u_start<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    sample_steps <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(t <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> dt): t <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.3<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>)}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> step <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(sample_steps) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> dt <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (u_final <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> u) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> tau<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> step <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> sample_steps:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sample_steps[step]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            exact <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u_final <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (u_start <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> u_final) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> tau)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>dt<span class=\"sc\">:.3f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> s | <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>t<span class=\"sc\">:.1f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> s | <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>u<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V | &quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                  <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>exact<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V | Error <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(u <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> exact)<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na step of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=10\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.953965<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.953965\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.901903<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.901903\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe exact comparison value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.896362<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.896362\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error occurs because the method holds the decreasing slope constant\nduring each step.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The RL Circuit as a Counterpart<\/h2>\n<p>In an RL series circuit, the inductor current is the storage\nvariable. For a source step to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_s=Ri_L+L\\,\\mathrm d i_L/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>L<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_L(t)=\\frac{U_s}{R}\n+\\left(i_L(0^-)-\\frac{U_s}{R}\\right)\\mathrm e^{-t/\\tau},\n\\qquad \\tau=\\frac LR.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=100\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=20\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=5\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the source rises to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith an initially current-free inductor, the current approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>126<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">126\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows. Immediately after the step, the entire source voltage appears\nacross the inductor. In the steady-state model, it appears entirely\nacross the resistor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A capacitor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>47<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">47\\,\\mu\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndischarges from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the time at which its voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows through an inductor immediately before the RL circuit described\nabove is switched to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat are the inductor voltage at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the current at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A measurement at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstill deviates from the final value by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36.8\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelative to what quantity is this statement valid?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>103.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC=103.4\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C/U_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>71.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau\\ln 2\\approx71.7\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The current remains at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L(0^+)=4\\,\\mathrm V-20\\,\\Omega\\cdot50\\,\\mathrm{mA}=3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubsequently,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>145<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_L(\\tau)=200\\,\\mathrm{mA}-150\\,\\mathrm{mA}/\\mathrm e\\approx145\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The reference quantity is the magnitude of the <strong>initial\ndeviation<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u_C(0^+)-u_\\infty|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor the corresponding current deviation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>First-order models apply when there is exactly one independent\nstorage variable and a constant linear network after switching. In a\nlarger linear resistive network, the effective resistance at the\nterminals of the storage element can be calculated as an equivalent\nresistance. If several independent storage elements are involved, the\ntime response may contain multiple decay components or oscillations.\nComponent tolerances and additional resistances alter measured time\nconstants.<\/p>\n<p>The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next\nlesson<\/a> uses the same component equations for sinusoidal excitation\nafter the transient has decayed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-5-rc-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.5 – RC Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.5 –\ncharging, discharging and the RC time constant · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 14.4 – RL Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 14.4 –\nRL step response and time constant · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 Circuits and Electronics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLecture 12 on capacitors and first-order systems · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M1-L6 – Sinusoidal Alternating Current and Complex Impedance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transient\nresponses<\/a>, sine and cosine, and arithmetic with complex numbers. If\nnecessary, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">review\ncomplex numbers in university mathematics<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Represent a sinusoidal voltage\nas an RMS phasor and calculate current and component voltages in a\nlinear AC circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Time-Domain Waveforms to Phasors<\/h2>\n<p>A capacitor can transmit rapid voltage changes differently from slow\nones. For the RC circuit in the <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous\nlesson<\/a>, we investigated the time response following a step. Now the\nsource remains sinusoidal indefinitely. In a stable linear network, the\ninfluence of initial conditions decays. What remains is the forced\nresponse at the same frequency. Its amplitude and phase may differ from\nthose of the source.<\/p>\n<p>With frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz and angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe write a voltage as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\,U_\\mathrm{eff}\\cos(\\omega t+\\varphi_u),\n\\qquad\n\\underline U=U_\\mathrm{eff}\\,\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\varphi_u},\n\\qquad\nu(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\left(\\underline U\\,\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\omega t}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j^2=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe phasor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the RMS value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{eff}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas its magnitude and the phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi_u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas its angle. For current, we use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder the same convention. The peak value of a sinusoid is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes its RMS value, because the mean of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover a period is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a calculation, all participating phasors share the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\omega t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the same convention for magnitudes.<\/p>\n<p>To convert between polar and Cartesian forms, use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\varphi}=A\\cos\\varphi+\\mathrm jA\\sin\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAddition is convenient in Cartesian form. When dividing two phasors,\ndivide their magnitudes and subtract their angles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Impedance of Resistors, Inductors and Capacitors<\/h2>\n<p>Under the passive sign convention, positive current enters at the\npositive voltage terminal. Differentiation with respect to time\ncorresponds to multiplying the phasor by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis follows from differentiating the common exponential factor. The\ncomponent laws thus become algebraic relations of the form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U=Z\\underline I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComplex <strong>impedance<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in ohms, like resistance.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th>Time domain<\/th>\n<th>Impedance for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Voltage phase relative to current<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=Ri<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_R=R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inductor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=L\\,\\mathrm di/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L=\\mathrm j\\omega L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=C\\,\\mathrm du/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_C=1/(\\mathrm j\\omega C)=-\\mathrm j/(\\omega C)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=R+\\mathrm jX<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called <strong>reactance<\/strong>. Its sign matters when adding\nimpedances. In a series circuit, impedances add because the same current\nflows everywhere. If branches are connected in parallel, the reciprocals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1/Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalled <strong>admittances<\/strong>, add for finite nonzero impedances.\nThis follows from the common voltage and the current law. Kirchhoff’s\noriginal laws therefore also apply to both complex components of the\nphasors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An RC Circuit at Its Cutoff Frequency<\/h2>\n<p>A source with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeeds a resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=100\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a capacitor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=1\\,\\mu\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin series. As the phase reference, the source phasor has an angle of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=1/(RC)=10^4\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.59<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\approx1.59\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_C=-\\mathrm j100\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(100-\\mathrm j100)\\,\\Omega,\n\\qquad\n\\underline I=\\frac{2\\,\\mathrm V}{Z}=(10+\\mathrm j10)\\,\\mathrm{mA}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The current has an RMS value of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.14\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand leads the source by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts time-domain waveform is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i(t)=20\\,\\mathrm{mA}\\cos(10^4\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\cdot t+45^\\circ).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the component voltages, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_R=(1+\\mathrm j)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U_C=(1-\\mathrm j)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nalthough both RMS values are individually\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nKirchhoff’s voltage law applies to phasors. Adding their magnitudes\nseparately would disregard the different phases.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_114_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Phasor diagram: resistor voltage one plus j volts, capacitor voltage one minus j volts, and a sum of two volts on the real axis.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Adding the 1 + j V and 1 − j V phasors cancels the\nimaginary parts, leaving 2 V along the real axis.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The capacitor-voltage phasor is also shown translated to the tip of\nthe resistor-voltage phasor without changing its direction. This\nillustrates addition according to the voltage law; the two imaginary\nparts cancel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal inductor with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=20\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=500\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a voltage with RMS value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat are the magnitude and phase of the current?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the RC example, what happens to the voltage across the capacitor\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero? What happens when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes very large?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reconstruct the time-domain function for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline U=-\\mathrm j3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=1000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L=\\mathrm j10\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline I=-\\mathrm j0.1\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe RMS value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe current phase lags the voltage by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At very low frequencies,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≫<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z_C|\\gg R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlmost the entire source voltage appears across the capacitor. At very\nhigh frequencies,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z_C|\\ll R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe capacitor voltage approaches zero. The output across the capacitor\ntherefore acts as a low-pass filter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the angle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)=3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm V\\cos(1000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\cdot t-90^\\circ)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>Phasors at a fixed frequency describe sinusoidal steady state. For\nthe switch-on transient, we still need initial conditions and\ntime-domain equations. Several frequencies can be treated separately in\na linear network and superposed in the time domain. Component\nconstruction and parasitic effects limit the applicability of ideal\ncomponent models at high frequencies.<\/p>\n<p>In the <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next\nlesson<\/a>, we use the same RMS phasors to calculate active power,\nreactive power and resonance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 Circuits and Electronics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLectures 16–18 on sinusoidal steady state, impedance and filters · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-2-simple-ac-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 15.2 – Simple AC Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n15.2 – individual components and phase relations · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-3-rlc-series-circuits-with-ac\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 15.3 – RLC Series Circuits with AC<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 15.3 – impedance and phase relations in RLC series circuits ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M1-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M1-L7 – AC Power, Frequency Response and Resonance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Bachelor: Linear Electrical Networks and Circuit Theory<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">RMS phasors\nand impedances<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">transient\nresponses<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish active, reactive and\napparent power, and determine the resonant frequency, frequency response\nand bandwidth of an idealised RLC series circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power in Sinusoidal Operation<\/h2>\n<p>The instantaneous power absorbed by a component is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=u(t)i(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen the current is defined according to the passive sign convention. At\nthe same angular frequency, we express voltage and current using their\n<strong>RMS values<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand their phases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi_u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\varphi_u-\\varphi_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cosine product identity gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=UI\\cos\\varphi\n+UI\\cos\\!\\left(2\\omega t+\\varphi_u+\\varphi_i\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second term averages to zero over one period. Thus the\n<strong>active power<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=UI\\cos\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin watts. Inductors and capacitors can temporarily absorb and return\nenergy. Their active power is zero in ideal sinusoidal operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complex Power and Power Factor<\/h2>\n<p>The RMS phasors from the previous lesson combine voltage, current and\nphase difference in<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline S=\\underline U\\,\\underline I^{*}=P+\\mathrm jQ,\n\\qquad P=UI\\cos\\varphi,\\qquad Q=UI\\sin\\varphi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The asterisk denotes the complex conjugate of the current phasor. It\nreverses the phase angle, so the product contains the phase difference.\n<strong>Reactive power<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis expressed in var. Under the passive sign convention it is positive\nfor inductive loads and negative for capacitive loads. The magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline S|=UI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>apparent power<\/strong> in volt-amperes. In the\nsinusoidal operation considered here, the power factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P/|\\underline S|=\\cos\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">UI&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A source with an RMS voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeeds a load with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(40+\\mathrm j30)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|=50\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.1\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline S=Z|\\underline I|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.40\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.30\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline S|=0.50\\,\\mathrm{VA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe power factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.8<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>RLC Series Circuit and Frequency Response<\/h2>\n<p>A sinusoidal source drives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin series. We consider the output <strong>across the resistor<\/strong>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R,L,C&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><msub><munder><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(\\omega)=R+\\mathrm j\\left(\\omega L-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right),\n\\qquad\nH_R(\\mathrm j\\omega)=\\frac{\\underline U_R}{\\underline U_\\mathrm{in}}\n=\\frac{R}{Z(\\omega)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The magnitude of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the ratio of RMS values. Its angle describes the phase shift\nof the output. At very low frequencies, the capacitor dominates the\nimpedance. At very high frequencies, the inductor dominates. In both\nlimits, the output amplitude tends to zero.<\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe reactances cancel. The input then sees\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe current is maximal,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H_R|=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand input and output are in phase. The output phase leads below\nresonance and lags above it. The voltage phasors across\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel in the loop sum. Their individual magnitudes can be\nsubstantial.<\/p>\n<p>Multiplying the numerator and denominator of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_R(\\mathrm j\\omega)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j\\omega/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreveals the structure of the transfer function:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> for the frequency response<\/mtext><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_R(s)=\\frac{(R/L)s}{s^2+(R/L)s+1/(LC)},\n\\qquad s=\\mathrm j\\omega\\text{ for the frequency response}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The quadratic denominator indicates a <strong>second-order\nsystem<\/strong>. The two independent storage variables are capacitor\nvoltage and inductor current. The resistor damps the free response. The\nresonant frequency of the forced current identifies a point on the\nfrequency response. The frequency of free damped oscillation\nadditionally depends on damping and is defined only when the system can\noscillate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bandwidth in a Worked Example<\/h2>\n<p>Let the source voltage have the same RMS value at all frequencies\nconsidered. At the two half-power frequencies, the active power across\nthe resistor falls to half its resonant value. Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R=I^2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H_R|=1/\\sqrt{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left|\\omega L-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right|=R.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand solving the two quadratic equations gives the positive solutions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2+(R/(2L))^2}-R/(2L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2+(R/(2L))^2}+R/(2L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\omega=\\omega_+-\\omega_-=R/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA larger resistance broadens the passband of this model.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=20\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=50\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=20\\,\\mu\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=1000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>159.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0\\approx159.2\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe resonant current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the active power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe half-power frequencies are approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>820<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">820<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1220<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1220\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">400\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>63.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">63.7\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_115_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Magnitude response with resonance at 1000 radians per second and half-power limits at approximately 820 and 1220 radians per second.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Resonance occurs at 1000 rad/s; the approximate\nhalf-power limits of 820 and 1220 rad/s give a bandwidth of about 400\nrad/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The frequency response makes the two half-power conditions visible.\nBandwidth is measured between the two marked frequencies, with the input\nvoltage held constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reverse the sign of the reactance of the load with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(40+\\mathrm j30)\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat changes if the RMS voltage remains constant?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the RLC example, calculate the magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H_R|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand determine the sign of the phase shift.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do the bandwidth and resonant current change if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled in the example?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><munder><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\underline S|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the power factor remain unchanged. The load becomes capacitive and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=-0.30\\,\\mathrm{var}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The inductor contributes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the capacitor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-25\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the reactance.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>75<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.258<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H_R|=20/\\sqrt{20^2+75^2}\\approx0.258<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output voltage lags the source.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The bandwidth doubles to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">800\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the resonant current falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremain unchanged, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains unchanged as well.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>The power formulas use sinusoidal RMS phasors at <strong>one\nfrequency<\/strong>. For distorted waveforms, for example, the time\naverage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the actual RMS values must be determined. The band-pass response\nshown applies to the series circuit with output across the resistor and\nno additional load at that output. Other output points lead to different\nfrequency responses. Losses and parasitic effects in real components\nalter the idealised curve.<\/p>\n<p>The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">study\npathway<\/a> lists the other course areas and their prerequisites.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 Circuits and Electronics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLectures 16–18 on sinusoidal steady state, impedances and filters · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-4-power-in-an-ac-circuit\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 15.4 – Power in an AC Circuit<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 15.4 – average power and power factor · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-5-resonance-in-an-ac-circuit\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 15.5 – Resonance in an AC Circuit<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 15.5 – series resonance and power curve · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
